1 720392","wd":"Q56558815","ad":"Market Street","wifi":1},{"n":"Feel Unique","g":"essentials","k":"pharmacy","la":49.454604,"lo":-2.537431,"d":363},{"n":"Co-Op Post Office","g":"essentials","k":"post_office","la":49.454107,"lo":-2.537468,"d":407},{"n":"Health Exchange","g":"essentials","k":"pharmacy","la":49.453366,"lo":-2.535946,"d":427},{"n":"St Julian's Pier","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":49.458611,"lo":-2.531944,"d":232,"ds":"St Julian's Pier is a pier in Saint Peter Port Harbour, Guernsey. The pier runs from the base of St Julian's Avenue, where a roundabout is now located, eastwards. The cambridge berth is a later construction built at right angles to the pier, in a southerly direction as is the New Jetty and at the eastern extremity, the White Rock Pier.","wpu":"St Julian's Pier","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/aa/Guernsey_July_2010_Plaque_51.jpg/960px-Guernsey_July_2010_Plaque_51.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Saint Peter Port Harbour","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":49.455556,"lo":-2.531111,"d":263,"ds":"Saint Peter Port Harbour is located in Saint Peter Port, Guernsey. It was a natural anchorage used by the Romans which has been changed into an artificial harbour that is now the island's main port for passengers. Loose cargo, liquids and gas are shipped to and from St Sampson's harbour.","wpu":"Saint Peter Port Harbour","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/48/Saint_Peter_Port_Harbour_%282014%29.jpg/960px-Saint_Peter_Port_Harbour_%282014%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Braye du Valle, Guernsey","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":49.487,"lo":-2.535,"d":3336,"ds":"The Braye du Valle is the area between the main Island of Guernsey and Le Clos du Valle, which was a tidal island to the north.","wpu":"Braye du Valle, Guernsey","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e4/Guernsey_and_Alderney_with_Island_of_Sark_1748.jpg/960px-Guernsey_and_Alderney_with_Island_of_Sark_1748.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Doyle Monument","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":49.4269,"lo":-2.5342,"d":3346,"ds":"The Doyle Monument is located in Jerbourg Point in the southeastern point of the Bailiwick of Guernsey within St Martin Parish. It was built to honour Sir John Doyle (1756-1834), Lieutenant Governor of Guernsey 1803-1816, by the people of the island.","wpu":"Doyle Monument","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e6/Guernsey_July_2011_170%2C_Doyle_Monument.jpg/960px-Guernsey_July_2011_170%2C_Doyle_Monument.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Vale Castle, Guernsey","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":49.485139,"lo":-2.509139,"d":3607,"ds":"Vale Castle, is a protected building located in the Vale, Guernsey. The original name was \"Le Château St Michel\", later it became \"Château de Val\" or \"Château de Valle\" and is over 1,000 years old. It defends both St.","wpu":"Vale Castle, Guernsey","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1a/Guernsey_July_2010_Vale_Castle_05.jpg/960px-Guernsey_July_2010_Vale_Castle_05.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Bréhon Tower","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":49.471222,"lo":-2.488139,"d":3672,"ds":"The Bréhon Tower (French pronunciation: [bʁeɔ̃]; or Fort Brehon) is accessible only by boat and sits on Bréhon Rock, an island in the Little Russell channel about 1.5 km northeast of St Peter Port, Guernsey, between the port and the islands of Herm and Jethou. Thomas Charles de Putron (1806–1869) built the oval tower of granite from Herm, completing the work in 1857.","wpu":"Bréhon Tower","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/08/Brehon_Tower.jpg/960px-Brehon_Tower.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Little Chapel","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":49.442564,"lo":-2.587661,"d":4198,"ds":"The Little Chapel is situated in the Les Vauxbelets valley, Saint Andrew, Guernsey. It was created in July 1914, by Brother Déodat. He planned to create a miniature version of the grotto and basilica at Lourdes, the Rosary Basilica.","wpu":"Little Chapel","img":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/3/34/Little_Chapel_op_Guernsey.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled"},{"n":"Icart Point","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":49.42,"lo":-2.56,"d":4523,"ds":"Icart Point is a point of southern Guernsey, located west of Jerbourg Point and east of Petit Port, Moulin Huet and Saint's Bay. It is in the parish of Saint Martin, Guernsey A German fortification Wildstandnest Ikart was built on the headland during 1942–3. Also on the headland is Saints Bay Hotel.","wpu":"Icart Point","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0d/Guernsey_July_2011_197.jpg/960px-Guernsey_July_2011_197.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Jethou","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":49.458333,"lo":-2.4625,"d":5169,"ds":"Jethou ( zheh-TOO) is a small island that is part of the Bailiwick of Guernsey in the Channel Islands. It is privately leased from the Crown, and not open to the public. Resembling the top of a wooded knoll, it is immediately southwest of Herm and covers approximately 44 acres (18 ha).","wpu":"Jethou","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/25/Jethou%2C_island_in_Channel_islands.jpg/960px-Jethou%2C_island_in_Channel_islands.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Widerstandsnest Rundturm","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":49.469272,"lo":-2.606328,"d":5402,"ds":"Widerstandsnest Rundturm (abbreviated as Wn. Rundturm; English: \"Round Tower Strongpoint\") was a World War II German coastal fortification located on the sea wall of Vazon Bay in Castel, Guernsey. Built by occupying German forces between 1941 and 1944, it was a part of Fortress Guernsey and Adolf Hitler's Atlantic Wall.","wpu":"Widerstandsnest Rundturm","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5c/Guernsey_July_2010_78%2C_Vazon_Bay_martello_tower.jpg/960px-Guernsey_July_2010_78%2C_Vazon_Bay_martello_tower.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"}],"about":{"ds":"St. Peter Port (French: Saint-Pierre Port) is a town and one of the ten parishes on the island of Guernsey in the Channel Islands. It is the capital of the Bailiwick of Guernsey as well as the main port.","wpu":"Saint Peter Port","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/87/Alb%C3%A8rt_%C3%89glyise_Saint_Pi%C3%A8rre_Port_Dg%C3%A8rn%C3%A9sy.jpg/960px-Alb%C3%A8rt_%C3%89glyise_Saint_Pi%C3%A8rre_Port_Dg%C3%A8rn%C3%A9sy.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},"wk":"2026-10-26"}